---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { REPO } from '../lib/paths';
---

<Base
  title="Methods"
  description="How Bantay Mina makes its before-and-after images and forest-loss figures, and what they cannot show."
>
  <article class="wrap prose">
    <header class="head">
      <p class="label">Methods</p>
      <h1>How the images and numbers are made</h1>
    </header>

    <p>
      Everything here comes from free, public data and one open-source script (<code>pipeline/</code> in the{' '}
      <a href={REPO}>project repository</a>). Anyone can rerun it and should get the same pictures and the same
      hectares.
    </p>

    <h2>Before-and-after images</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Same box, same grid.</strong> Each site has one square analysis box, 4 to 16 km wide
        depending on how far the mine spreads, on a 10 m UTM grid (20 m for Zambales). Every image
        of that site is drawn on exactly that grid, so the slider compares the same
        ground pixel for pixel.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Same months, chosen from the cloud record.</strong> Both sides of a comparison use scenes from
        the same months of the year, so a wet-season "before" is never set against a dry-season "after" and
        normal browning or green-up is not mistaken for damage. The months are chosen per site from the
        Sentinel-2 cloud record for 2019–2025: for each run of months, the share of the box that had at least
        three cloud-free views in every one of those years. In Luzon and on Semirara, January–May scores best
        (97–100% of the box in the cloudiest year), which is the dry season there. Eastern Mindanao and the
        Dinagat Islands have no real dry season and are cloudiest from November to March, so the Caraga sites
        use April–September (87–100%). Toledo, in the Cebu uplands, uses March–August (99%). Each image label
        states its months.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Many scenes, not one.</strong> Clouds and their shadows are removed with each satellite's own
        quality mask (Landsat <code>QA_PIXEL</code>, Sentinel-2 scene classification). Those masks miss some
        thin and bright cloud, and missed cloud is almost always brighter than the ground. So for each pixel the
        pipeline ranks its remaining clear views by blue brightness and keeps the one a quarter of the way up
        from the darkest, all bands from that same view. The caption under each slider lists how many
        acquisition dates were used and what share of the box had at least one clear view; grey pixels had none.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>True colour, no boosting.</strong> Red, green and blue surface reflectance, with one fixed
        brightness stretch applied identically to both sides. Nothing is recoloured to look worse.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Labels burned in.</strong> Each image carries its date range, satellite, resolution and
        attribution, so a screenshot shared on its own still says what it is. Unlabelled GeoTIFF copies are
        linked under every slider for checking in QGIS.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Landsat (30 m) goes back to the 1980s and is used when the "before" date predates Sentinel-2. It is
      resampled to the 10 m grid for display; that makes it smoother, not sharper. Sentinel-2 (10 m) starts in
      2015–2017 and shows more detail.
    </p>

    <h2>Vegetation → bare ground</h2>
    <p>
      For each pair of images, the pipeline also computes the vegetation index NDVI (from the red and
      near-infrared bands) of the same chosen views. A pixel counts as "went from green vegetation to
      bare ground or water" if its NDVI was at least 0.5 before (dense green cover) and below 0.3 after (bare
      soil, rock, water, pavement or very sparse cover). The sea, rivers and lakes that were already water in
      the 2000s (Hansen data mask) are left out, because open water gives noisy NDVI; ponds dug since then,
      such as tailings ponds, are counted. Pixels without a clear view on both sides are also left out. The
      magenta overlay on each slider shows exactly which pixels were counted.
    </p>
    <p>
      This catches clearing of farms, brush and regrowth that tree cover datasets miss. It also catches
      anything else that turns green land bare, such as new roads, landslides or buildings, which is why the
      figure inside the mapped mine footprint is given separately. It undercounts where the land was dry grass
      or brush before, because that is not dense green in the dry season. The thresholds are fixed for every
      site and are not tuned to make a site look worse.
    </p>

    <h2>Water change</h2>
    <p>
      The same chosen views also give the water index NDWI (from the green and near-infrared bands). A pixel
      "turned from land into water" if its NDWI was below −0.1 before and above 0.1 after, and the reverse for
      "turned from water into land". Open sea is so dark that its NDWI is unreliable, so inside water mapped in
      the 2000s (Hansen data mask) any pixel darker than 0.03 in near-infrared counts as water; on land, dark
      terrain shadow does not. "Dry" land must reflect at least 0.05, and only ground that was land in the 2000s
      can count as newly water. The gap between the thresholds keeps
      wet soil, shadow and muddy
      shallows from flickering into change. This picks up new ponds, such as tailings impoundments and flooded
      pits, and shorelines or river mouths that moved. The blue and lime overlay shows which pixels were
      counted. Tides, river floods and seasonal ponds also move water, so one comparison on its own does not
      prove that mining caused the change.
    </p>

    <h2>Tree cover loss</h2>
    <p>
      Hectares of tree cover loss come from the Hansen / University of Maryland Global Forest Change dataset
      (version GFC-2025-v1.13, 30 m, yearly 2001–2025), the same data behind Global Forest Watch. A pixel
      counts as tree cover if it had at least 30% canopy in 2000, the Global Forest Watch default. Pixel areas
      are computed from latitude.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Loss has many causes</strong>: mining, but also farming, roads, landslides and typhoons. The
      figure for the whole box is therefore labelled "all causes". The figure inside the mapped mine footprint
      is the one most directly tied to mining.
    </p>

    <h2>Mine footprints</h2>
    <p>
      Outlines of pits, waste dumps, tailings ponds and processing plants come from Tang &amp; Werner (2023),
      who traced 74,548 mine features worldwide by hand on high-resolution satellite imagery. They cover what
      was visible when that imagery was captured, so newer expansion may be missing, and they are not permit
      boundaries.
    </p>

    <h2 id="protected-areas">Protected areas</h2>
    <p>
      Where a site's box reaches a legally protected area, the slider has a layer for its boundary (green) and,
      if the law sets one, its buffer zone (dashed). The figure under the slider says how far the mapped mine
      footprint is from the boundary, and how much of the green-to-bare ground lies inside the area and its
      buffer. That second figure counts all clearing, so the page text says what it is.
    </p>
    <p>
      Boundaries come from the law itself when it prints coordinates. The Northern Sierra Madre Natural Park
      is rebuilt from the 81 corner points in Section 4 of
      <a href="https://lawphil.net/statutes/repacts/ra2001/ra_9125_2001.html">RA 9125</a>. Two of them are
      plainly misprinted, in the law and in Proclamation 978 (1997) before it. Point 15's longitude reads
      122°59′31″, about 105 km east of its neighbours, which makes the boundary cross itself; point 41's
      latitude reads 16°21′28″, about 55 km south of its neighbours. Read as 121°59′31″ and 16°51′28″, the
      boundary closes cleanly. The law's coordinates are on the old Luzon 1911 datum. Converted to WGS84, the
      boundary matches the Biodiversity Management Bureau's polygon of the park (Geoportal Philippines) to
      within 36 metres, and encloses 356,980 ha against the 359,486 ha the law states. For areas whose law
      gives no coordinates, the BMB polygon is used directly. Every boundary is checked against the area its
      law states. These are drawn for public reference, not as a legal survey.
    </p>

    <h2>What this site does not claim</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Satellite images show land cover. They do not show water contamination, health effects, or whether an
        operation is legal. Those claims appear only in the text, each with a cited source.
      </li>
      <li>
        Mining permit boundaries (MGB tenement maps) are not yet on the map. Until they are, the site does not
        say whether clearing happened inside or outside a permit.
      </li>
      <li>
        No homes, villages or individuals are mapped. Indigenous communities are named only as they are named
        in public sources.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Data and licences</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Landsat Collection 2 Level-2: USGS/NASA, public domain.</li>
      <li>Sentinel-2 Level-2A: contains modified Copernicus Sentinel data, free and open licence.</li>
      <li>Both accessed through Microsoft Planetary Computer.</li>
      <li>
        Hansen et al. 2013, <em>Science</em> 342: 850–853; data CC BY 4.0.
      </li>
      <li>
        Tang, L. &amp; Werner, T. T. 2023, <em>Communications Earth &amp; Environment</em> 4: 134; data CC BY 4.0.
      </li>
      <li>Basemap: © OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL), tiles by OpenFreeMap.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="corrections">Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      If a date, figure or source is wrong, <a href={`${REPO}/issues`}>open an issue</a> on the project
      repository with a link to the evidence. Corrections are made openly and noted on the affected page with
      the date they were made.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .head {
    padding: 2.2rem 0 1rem;
  }
  .head .label {
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }
  code {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.85em;
  }
</style>
